\viewkind4\uc1\margl720\margr720\margt1440\margb1440
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs32\b newVM FFT First-Call vs Steady-State Benchmark (N=8192)\b0\fs22\par
\fs24\b Follow-up to perf/newVMCLperformance.rtf: isolating first-call setup cost from steady-state transform cost\b0\par
\b Host: newVMComplexSingle.pas (FFTW3)  vs  GPU: newVMCL.pas (clFFT), single complex FFT/IFFT, N=8192, Linux / x86_64\b0\par
\fs22\par

\b Machine:\b0  Intel(R) Core(TM) i7-14650HX (hybrid: 8 P-cores/16 threads + 8 E-cores/8 threads = 24 logical CPUs), 31 GiB RAM\par
\b OS:\b0  Ubuntu 24.04.4 LTS, kernel 7.0.0-30-generic\par
\b GPU:\b0  NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5070 Laptop GPU, 8151 MiB VRAM, driver 595.84, OpenCL 3.0 (CUDA 13.2.87), 36 compute units - same device perf/newVMCLperformance.rtf's platform-selection fix directed {\f1 newVMCL.pas}\f0  to use\par
\b Compiler flags:\b0  fpc -Fu. -Fi. newVMCLfft8192bench.lpr (plain fpc build, default optimisation, assertions off)\par
\fs22\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs24\b Motivation:\b0  perf/newVMCLperformance.rtf's FFT/IFFT table showed GPU forward-FFT flat at roughly 190-380ms across every N from 1024 to 16384 - not scaling with size the way a real transform should - and attributed this to newVMCL.pas creating and destroying a fresh clFFT plan on every call, with the actual JIT kernel-compile cost suspected (but not confirmed) to dominate. That report only ever called FFT/IFFT ONCE per N, so it could not separate a one-time setup cost from a genuine per-call cost. This follow-up narrows to a single size (N=8192, the middle of that report's range) and measures each side's very FIRST FFT/IFFT call separately from the average (plus min/max) of 100 REPEAT calls that follow, via a new standalone program, newVMCLfft8192bench.lpr - see that file's own header comment for the full protocol. No newVM library code changed for this follow-up (only the new benchmark program was added); perf/newVMCLperformance.rtf's 292-test suite verification still applies unchanged.\par
\fs22\par

\pard\page

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs26\b Results\b0\fs22\par
\pard\sa0\sl240\slmult1\f1\fs20
Metric                   First(ms)    Avg100(ms)   Min(ms)   Max(ms)\par
Host FFT                     0.954        0.0734    0.0700    0.1660\par
Host IFFT                    0.286        0.0723    0.0710    0.0900\par
GPU FFT (warm cache)         5.766        1.3731    1.0000    4.7210\par
GPU IFFT (warm cache)        1.285        1.0413    0.9960    1.3340\par
GPU FFT (cold cache)       271.869        1.3350    1.0050    4.5600\par
GPU IFFT (cold cache)        4.500        1.3762    1.0000    4.5590\par
\pard\sa100\sl240\slmult1\f0\fs16 (RoundTripResidual, all four runs: Host 1.563E-005, GPU 1.694E-005 - both correct; see Notes)\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22
\b "warm cache" vs "cold cache":\b0  NVIDIA's OpenCL/CUDA driver persists JIT-compiled kernel binaries to an on-disk cache ({\f1 ~/.nv/ComputeCache}\f0 , keyed by kernel source + device, independent of and outliving any single process) - so a program's own "first" GPU call is only genuinely cold if nothing has compiled that exact kernel on this machine before. This machine's cache already held N=8192's clFFT kernels from perf/newVMCLperformance.rtf's earlier run today, so a plain first-run measurement ("warm cache" row above) understates true cold-start cost. To measure the real thing, the cold-cache row was produced by rerunning {\f1 newVMCLfft8192bench}\f0  with {\f1 CUDA_CACHE_PATH}\f0  pointed at a freshly-created empty directory (an environment-variable override, not a deletion of the real cache) - forcing a genuine from-scratch compile.\par
\fs22\par

\pard\page

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs26\b What this confirms\b0\fs22\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22
1) \b The first-call cost is real, one-time, and almost entirely forward-transform JIT compilation - not a per-call cost, and not evenly split between directions.\b0  Cold GPU-FFT first call: 271.9ms. Cold GPU-IFFT first call (immediately after, same process, same N): only 4.5ms - a ~60x difference between directions on a machine where NEITHER has ever compiled this N before. So the earlier report's "IFFT is fast right after a slow FFT" observation was NOT primarily a caching artifact of calling FFT first, as that report's own hedged explanation guessed - clFFT's backward-transform kernel for this configuration is intrinsically far cheaper to JIT-compile than the forward one on this GPU/driver. That asymmetry is now a confirmed, direct finding rather than a guess from an indirect pattern.\par
2) \b The 271.9ms cold-compile figure lines up almost exactly with perf/newVMCLperformance.rtf's own N=8192 GPU-FFT number (271.11ms)\b0  - strong corroborating evidence that every per-N row in that report's table was, in effect, a cold first-ever-compile measurement for that N (that report only ever called each N once), not a stable "steady-state GPU FFT cost" as a casual reading might have suggested.\par
3) \b Once compiled - whether within the same process (the repeat-100 loop) or from an earlier process via the disk cache (the "warm cache" first-call row) - GPU FFT/IFFT settle to a stable, fast steady state:\b0  roughly 1.0-1.4ms per call at N=8192 (with an occasional ~4.5-4.7ms outlier in both min/max columns, itself consistent with an occasional background driver/scheduling hiccup rather than a repeat compile - it appears once per 100 calls, not on a fixed subset). {\f1 newVMCL.pas}\f0  creating and destroying a plan OBJECT on every call (as newVMCL.pas's source genuinely does) does not, in practice, force a recompile every time - the underlying compiled binary is reused by the driver across plan objects, processes, and even (via the disk cache) across separate runs of the whole program.\par
4) \b Host (FFTW3) is faster at every stage, and the gap does not close even at steady state.\b0  Host repeat-average FFT (0.0734ms) is roughly 18x faster than GPU repeat-average FFT (1.335-1.373ms) at this single size; IFFT similarly ~14x. This is an expected, not a bug: a single 8192-point complex FFT is a small amount of work (kernel-launch/queue-dispatch overhead alone plausibly explains most of the GPU's ~1ms floor), and GPUs win on this kind of operation through either much larger transforms or batching many transforms together, neither of which this benchmark (or perf/newVMCLperformance.rtf's) exercises - {\f1 newVMCL.pas}\f0  has no batched-transform entry point today.\par
\fs22\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22\b Notes:\b0\par
RoundTripResidual = {\f1 ||IFFT(FFT(x))-x||}\f0  (Frobenius/L2 norm), computed once from each side's first call; both sides land at ~1.6-1.7E-005, consistent with single-precision summation error at N=8192 and matching perf/newVMCLperformance.rtf's own N=8192 round-trip figures almost exactly (1.6E-005 host / 1.7E-005 GPU there too) - both builds compute the correct answer throughout this follow-up as well. Avg100/Min/Max are over 100 repeat calls following the first (i.e. 101 total calls per direction per condition); First(ms) is the timing of call number 1, reported separately rather than folded into the average, per this report's own purpose.\par
Cold-cache methodology: {\f1 CUDA_CACHE_PATH}\f0  is an NVIDIA driver environment variable redirecting where compiled-kernel binaries are cached on disk; pointing it at an empty, newly-created directory for one run - rather than clearing the user's real {\f1 ~/.nv/ComputeCache}\f0  - gives a genuinely cold compile without disturbing this machine's normal cache state for any other program. The directory was freshly created immediately before that run and not reused afterwards.\par
\fs22\par

\pard\page

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs26\b Summary\b0\fs22\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22
This follow-up confirms the hypothesis behind it: perf/newVMCLperformance.rtf's flat, non-scaling ~190-380ms GPU-FFT figures were indeed first-call setup cost, not per-call plan-creation overhead or per-call data-copy cost as such - specifically, clFFT's own forward-transform JIT compilation for that (N, device) pair, which the NVIDIA driver then caches to disk and reuses across every later call and every later process, this benchmark included. Backward-transform (IFFT) JIT compilation is far cheaper (~4.5ms even cold, vs forward's ~272ms) - a genuine, confirmed asymmetry in clFFT/the driver for this configuration, not a benchmark artifact. Once past that one-time cost, GPU FFT/IFFT at N=8192 run at a stable ~1.0-1.4ms per call - but that steady-state number is still roughly 14-18x slower than host FFTW3 at this single size, since a lone 8192-point transform is too small an amount of work to amortise GPU dispatch overhead against; the earlier report's broader conclusion (that {\f1 newVMCL.pas}\f0 's FFT path needs plan caching, and more generally batching, to be worth using for this codebase's real SDR use case, per {\f1 newVMCL.pas}\f0 's own header comment) stands and is now on firmer, directly-measured ground rather than an untested guess.\par
}
